Scott’s elementary account of a domain of domains starts from the powerset information system \(\mathbf{P}\) whose tokens are the non-negative integers, with \(0\) as \(\Delta\), every finite set consistent, and the least entailment, and then retains the approximable maps \(r:\mathbf{P}\to\mathbf{P}\) satisfying (1) \(I_{\mathbf{P}}\subseteq r\), (2) \(r\circ r=r\), (3) \(\overline{0}\subseteq r(\bot)\), (4) \(\top=r(\overline{1})\), and (5) \(\overline{1}\not\subseteq r(\overline{0})\), asserting that these maps are the elements of a domain cut out of \(\mathbf{P}\to\mathbf{P}\) in the same manner as \(\mathbf{U}\) was cut out of \(\mathbf{V}\); a finite set \(u\) is consistent for such an \(r\) when \(\overline{1}\not\subseteq r(\overline{u})\). `P` is that system, with bottom token `0`, `Con` the universally true predicate (`P.AllConsistent`), and `PEnt` the clause `n = 0 ∨ n ∈ u`, the six information-system axioms being the direct checks in `P`. `bar n` is the singleton, `barElement n` is its entailment closure, and `mem_barElement` says membership is `x = 0 ∨ x = n`; `botElement` is the closure of \(\emptyset\), `mem_botElement` says its only token is `0`, and `barElement_zero_eq_bot` identifies \(\overline{0}\) with \(\bot\). `ReflApprox` is inclusion of `idMap` relations, `IdemApprox` is equality `comp r r = r`, `BotApprox` is inclusion of the carrier of `barElement 0` in `r.toElement botElement`, `RogueApprox` is `r.toElement (barElement 1) = topElement`, and `ConsistentApprox` is the negation of the corresponding inclusion of `barElement 1`. `IsDomainApprox` is the conjunction of these five and `DomainApprox` is the subtype of maps that satisfy it. `scottCon r u` is failure of that inclusion for `P.closure u`, and `scottCon_empty_iff_consistent` is definitional once \(\overline{0}=\bot\). `BotApprox_any` shows (3) of every approximable self-map, by monotonicity from `empty_rel` up to entailment of `{0}`; `not_RogueApprox_idMap` shows (4) of the identity, because the token `2` cannot lie in an identity image of \(\overline{1}\). The exported `theorem_84` is only that negative statement. The inhabitant `flatMap` uses `flatRel`, which accepts every codomain set when `1 ∈ u` and otherwise requires every token of `v` to be `0` or a member of `u`, with `1 ∉ v`. Its approximable-map proof splits on those clauses; `flatMap.refl` feeds an identity entailment into them; `flatMap.idem` uses the intermediate set `insert 1 v` when `1 ∈ u` and `v` itself otherwise; `flatMap.rogue` applies `flatMap.toElement_of_mem_one` because `1` lies in `barElement 1`; and `flatMap.consistent` shows no finite set inside `barElement 0` is flat-related to `{1}`. `flatDomainApprox` only packages that inhabitant. The one-one correspondence of elements of \(\mathbf{P}\) with arbitrary sets of integers, the construction of an information system whose elements are the maps satisfying (1)–(5), and the argument through subdomains of \(\mathbf{U}\) are not proved; Scott himself postpones the passage from domain constructs to approximable maps. **Theorem:** partial — `IsDomainApprox` and `DomainApprox` restate (1)–(5) as a subtype of approximable self-maps of `P`, but that subtype is not shown to be the elements of a domain derived from \(\mathbf{P}\to\mathbf{P}\), and `theorem_84` asserts only that `idMap P` fails `RogueApprox`. **Proof:** partial — the five checks for `flatMap` follow the two clauses of `flatRel`, with (3) true of every approximable map by `BotApprox_any`, while the identification of these maps with all entailment relations on the integers, and the subdomain-of-\(\mathbf{U}\) sketch, are not proved.
